---
/**
 * SpecimenApparatus - the apparatus family, printed live.
 *
 * ─── WHAT CAN BE MOUNTED, AND WHAT CAN ONLY BE STATED ─────────────────────
 * Five of the six motion blocks are `<details>` disclosures: they print with no
 * script and the browser owns their state, so the sheet mounts them for real.
 * `MotionOrigin` is the family's one island - a Radix popover - and it is mounted
 * with `client:visible`, exactly as `/Motion` mounts it, because a drawing of a
 * trigger/surface relation would be a drawing of something the reader can do.
 *
 * The providers are different in kind: `AnalyticsProvider`, `Auth0Provider` and
 * `PageviewTracker` render no markup at all. A row cannot mount a context, so the
 * runtime rows print the CONTRACT each one exposes - the same keys its consumers
 * read - and say so in the row's `when` line. That is not a picture of a
 * component; it is the only honest form a context has on a printed sheet.
 *
 * The head is the third case: `MetaTags` writes into `<head>` and cannot be
 * mounted in a body. Its rows call `TagsOf()` - the function the head itself
 * renders from - and print the tags it returns as a register. The sheet is
 * therefore showing the head's real output, not a copy of it.
 *
 * ─── THE RENDERER DECIDES NOTHING ────────────────────────────────────────
 * A row states what it is, which part it exercises, when that state is right and
 * the arm it prints; this file builds the data and mounts the block. That split
 * is why the matrix and the rows cannot drift: both read
 * `Content/Specimen/Apparatus.ts`, and a state added there appears on both with
 * no second edit.
 *
 * ─── THE DATA IS THE SITE'S OWN ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * The motion classes are `Content/Motion/Register.ts`'s own records, the sheet's
 * cells are `Parts.ts`'s, the material is the token sheet's, the gallery is the
 * families' own exports, the trail crumbs are routes the site has, and the
 * article is the blog collection's shape. A specimen printed with lorem ipsum
 * would prove that the block can hold text; a specimen printed with the
 * document's own facts proves that the block can hold THIS document.
 *
 * ─── THE STAGE IS A PLATE, NOT A VIEWPORT ────────────────────────────────
 * The motion specimen splits 4/1px/7 above 1024px and the sequence runs
 * horizontally above 768px: both are viewport rules, and a plate that simulated
 * them would be printing a browser. The article is the third case and the only
 * one that is not a viewport's: its workspace splits into desk / paper / desk /
 * 1px / margin above 84rem of its own container, so the stage inherits whatever
 * composition its width earns. `_Apparatus.scss` declares the stacked
 * composition for the narrow stage and says so where it does it.
 */
